        &lt;span class="n"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Second&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;strconv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Atoi&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Header&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Retry-After"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Second&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Sleep&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The health request carries no application credential; the reporting request uses the telemetry API key. Its observation should be keyed by service, region, and deployment rather than by a learner. Keep the key out of &lt;code&gt;event.json&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Can you reconstruct the incident from retained evidence?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Test that question before calling the dashboard finished. Pick a synthetic incident window and ask an engineer who did not build the dashboard to explain the sequence. They should be able to identify the first external failure, the first internal readiness transition, the deployment in service, the affected region, and the recovery time. If they cannot, collecting another dozen host metrics will not repair the missing chronology.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a lesson-publishing incident, a useful sequence might contain five records: deployment started, readiness became true, publishing errors rose, readiness became false because the critical datastore check timed out, and an external probe failed from one region. These are event types, not fabricated measurements. Your production values must come from the system itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Clock handling can quietly ruin the exercise. Emit UTC timestamps, preserve the probe observation time, and account for known clock skew when ordering events from separate machines. Keep deployment IDs consistent across logs, metrics, and health output. Retain enough history to cover the organization's incident-review window and privacy policy; there is no universal number of days.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do the drill twice: once for a dependency failure, and once for a silent scheduler failure. The latter exposes a blind spot. A healthy web process cannot prove that yesterday's lesson-notification job ran. Healthchecks.io and similar dead-man's-switch services are built around expected check-ins, while an external uptime probe tests the network path. Both answer questions an in-process Express route cannot.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Choosing the monitoring path
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No single option owns this design. Prometheus is a strong fit when a team already operates pull-based metrics and Alertmanager; its data model supports the gauge and counters described above, but the team owns scraping, retention, and the operational path. Datadog combines synthetic tests, logs, metrics, and alerting in a managed platform, which reduces integration work but increases dependence on its agent, data model, and service.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Better Stack offers hosted uptime checks and incident-management workflows, making it useful for the external perspective. Healthchecks.io is narrower and particularly apt for "the scheduled task never ran." It should complement the web probes rather than replace them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrai fits an internal, lightweight path because its one plain REST API and one key span its backend capabilities, with one bill instead of separate credentials and reconciliation for adjacent work; any component that can send HTTP can report logs and metrics without installing a product-specific SDK or tracking its versions. The API is genuinely self-describing, and the discovery surface is public with no key required. Every documented capability ships runnable examples in 10 languages, while live discovery reports 295 routes across 20 modules. In this workflow, those traits let an integration validate the current ingest schema before deployment instead of pinning another client package.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Its limitations are decisive in some environments. Infrai has no alert or notification route, no heartbeat probing, and no distributed-trace query or span tree. Query filter parameters for log search and metrics query are not declared in discovery, so validate query behavior before making a dashboard contract depend on particular filters. Teams that want managed synthetic tests and notification policy in one product should choose Datadog or Better Stack; teams that need dead-man's-switch semantics should add Healthchecks.io. A polling alert worker plus a dedicated external probe is required for the Infrai path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That comparison yields a practical decision rule. Choose Prometheus when operating the metrics stack is acceptable; Datadog when consolidated managed telemetry and synthetics justify the coupling; Better Stack for a hosted external path; Healthchecks.io for scheduled-job silence; and a REST reporting surface when low client-library overhead matters and you are prepared to own alert evaluation. Mixed systems are normal.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The threshold can create its own outage
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A probe that pages on one failed request is fast and noisy. A probe that waits through a long window is calm and late. Neither threshold is a best practice independent of traffic, regional topology, deployment behavior, and the error budget.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Begin with an explicit policy: probe from at least the locations that represent materially different learner paths, require repeated failures before paging, and separate a single-region warning from a multi-region page. Measure what this policy would have done against real probe history before enabling notification. There is a concrete cost on both sides. False positives train the on-call to distrust pages and interrupt work; false negatives extend student impact and erase the earliest evidence from short-retention views.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also keep &lt;code&gt;/ready&lt;/code&gt; cheap. If every platform poll performs several sequential dependency calls, the health system adds load exactly when a dependency is weak. Cache recent bounded check results, apply timeouts shorter than the platform probe deadline, and expose the age of the observation. &lt;strong&gt;The threshold and check budget belong in the runbook alongside the reason for choosing them.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Close the loop after each incident review. Ask which signal first contained actionable evidence, which fields were missing, and whether the page led to a safe action. Change the instrumentation before adding another dashboard panel. The target is a defensible timeline from learner-visible failure to readiness transition to recovery, with enough evidence to explain what happened and no claim that a green process check proves end-to-end availability.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Further reading
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Express production best practices: health checks and graceful shutdown: &lt;a href="https://expressjs.com/en/advanced/healthcheck-graceful-shutdown.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://expressjs.com/en/advanced/healthcheck-graceful-shutdown.html&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Kubernetes liveness, readiness, and startup probes: &lt;a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/liveness-readiness-startup-probes/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/liveness-readiness-startup-probes/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;OpenTelemetry metrics signal concepts: &lt;a href="https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/metrics/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/metrics/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Prometheus metric types: &lt;a href="https://prometheus.io/docs/concepts/metric_types/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://prometheus.io/docs/concepts/metric_types/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Datadog API tests: &lt;a href="https://docs.datadoghq.com/synthetics/api_tests/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://docs.datadoghq.com/synthetics/api_tests/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Better Stack uptime monitoring: &lt;a href="https://betterstack.com/docs/uptime/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://betterstack.com/docs/uptime/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Healthchecks.io documentation: &lt;a href="https://healthchecks.io/docs/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://healthchecks.io/docs/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;RFC 5424, The Syslog Protocol: &lt;a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5424" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5424&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  Extending PcDevice Search: Regex Limits and Invoice Fields Integration
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; I tightened the collaborator‑number regex to accept only 3‑6 digits and added &lt;code&gt;invoiceNumber&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;purchaseOrder&lt;/code&gt; fields to the &lt;code&gt;PcDevice&lt;/code&gt; model, making them searchable and visible in the device detail sheet. The changes touch the Prisma schema, the global search logic, the device store hook, and the UI component that renders device details.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The internal inventory tool (&lt;code&gt;pcview&lt;/code&gt;) allowed users to search for collaborator numbers without any length restriction. This caused false positives when a user typed a long numeric string (e.g., a ticket ID) that matched the loose &lt;code&gt;/^\d{3,}$/&lt;/code&gt; pattern. Additionally, the product team requested that purchase information—&lt;code&gt;invoiceNumber&lt;/code&gt; (factura) and &lt;code&gt;purchaseOrder&lt;/code&gt; (ODC)—be stored on each &lt;code&gt;PcDevice&lt;/code&gt; and be searchable, but the schema and UI did not expose these fields.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Symptoms:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Searching “1234567” (7 digits) returned collaborator results even though collaborator numbers are max 6 digits.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No way to query devices by invoice or purchase order.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The device detail sheet never displayed purchase metadata, forcing users to cross‑reference external spreadsheets.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first instinct was to add a front‑end length check inside &lt;code&gt;GlobalSearch.tsx&lt;/code&gt; before invoking the regex. I wrote:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;isCollaboratorNumber&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;But this introduced a race condition: the check ran after the regex, still allowing the long string to be processed and logged as an error in the backend. Moreover, it didn’t solve the core issue—&lt;strong&gt;the regex itself was too permissive&lt;/strong&gt;—and it duplicated validation logic across the UI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the invoice fields, I attempted a quick schema migration by adding raw SQL columns directly in the migration script, bypassing Prisma. That worked for the DB, but the TypeScript types in the codebase remained unaware of the new columns, causing compile‑time errors in &lt;code&gt;use-devices-store.ts&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;DeviceDetailSheet.tsx&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both approaches failed to be maintainable or type‑safe, so I reverted the changes and tackled the problem from the source: the schema and the shared search utility.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  1. Tightening the Collaborator Number Regex
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;File: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;src/features/search/GlobalSearch.tsx&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;@@ -58,7 +58,9 @@&lt;/span&gt; interface CollaboratorAssets {
 }
&lt;span class="err"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gd"&gt;-const isCollaboratorNumber = (q: string) =&amp;gt; /^\d{3,}$/.test(q.trim());
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+// Collaborator numbers are strictly 3‑6 digits.
+// Reject longer numeric strings that belong to other domains (e.g., ticket IDs).
+const isCollaboratorNumber = (q: string) =&amp;gt; /^\d{3,6}$/.test(q.trim());
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Why?&lt;/strong&gt; The updated regex enforces the business rule directly at the validation layer, eliminating the need for UI‑side length guards.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Impact:&lt;/strong&gt; The search dispatcher now correctly classifies queries like &lt;code&gt;123456&lt;/code&gt; as collaborator numbers and ignores &lt;code&gt;1234567&lt;/code&gt;, falling back to generic device search.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  2. Extending the Prisma Model
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;File: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;prisma/schema.prisma&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;@@ -63,6 +63,12 @@&lt;/span&gt; model PcDevice {
   // en el sync normal (no está en RawPcDevice).
   collaboratorNumber String?
&lt;span class="err"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  // Purchase metadata (manual enrichment from INVENTARIO)
+  invoiceNumber      String?  // Factura
+  purchaseOrder      String?  // ODC (Orden de Compra)
+
&lt;/span&gt;   // Additional fields omitted for brevity
 }
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Migration:&lt;/strong&gt; Ran &lt;code&gt;npx prisma migrate dev --name add-invoice-fields&lt;/code&gt; which generated the appropriate ALTER TABLE statements.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Type Safety:&lt;/strong&gt; Prisma now includes &lt;code&gt;invoiceNumber&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;purchaseOrder&lt;/code&gt; in the generated &lt;code&gt;PcDevice&lt;/code&gt; type, propagating through the entire TypeScript stack.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  3. Updating the Device Store Hook
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;File: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;src/hooks/use-devices-store.ts&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;@@ -27,6 +27,8 @@&lt;/span&gt; export interface PcDeviceRow {
   assignedTechnician: string | null;
   custodyNote: string | null;
   collaboratorNumber: string | null;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  invoiceNumber: string | null;
+  purchaseOrder: string | null;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rationale:&lt;/strong&gt; The hook pulls rows from the Prisma client and maps them to UI‑friendly objects. Adding the two fields ensures they travel from the DB to the UI without extra mapping logic.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  4. Making the New Fields Searchable
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The global search service builds a dynamic Prisma query based on the query type. I added a branch for purchase metadata:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/features/search/GlobalSearch.tsx (excerpt)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isInvoiceNumber&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;prisma&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pcDevice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;findMany&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;invoiceNumber&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;contains&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;mode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;insensitive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;deviceSelect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The helper &lt;code&gt;isInvoiceNumber&lt;/code&gt; mirrors the collaborator logic:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isInvoiceNumber&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="sr"&gt;/^&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;A-Z0-9&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;]{5,12}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;$/i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;trim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;());&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Simple alphanumeric pattern&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  5. Displaying Purchase Info in the Detail Sheet
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;File: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;src/features/devices/DeviceDetailSheet.tsx&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;@@ -37,6 +37,13 @@&lt;/span&gt; interface DeviceDetail {
   lastContactTime: string | null;
   department: string | null;
   isOnline: boolean;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  assignedUserName: string | null;
+  deliveryStatus: string | null;
+  // New fields
+  invoiceNumber: string | null;
+  purchaseOrder: string | null;
&lt;/span&gt; }
&lt;span class="err"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt; // Rendering block (added near the bottom of the sheet)
 {device.invoiceNumber &amp;amp;&amp;amp; (
   &amp;lt;DetailRow label="Invoice (Factura)" value={device.invoiceNumber} /&amp;gt;
 )}
 {device.purchaseOrder &amp;amp;&amp;amp; (
   &amp;lt;DetailRow label="Purchase Order (ODC)" value={device.purchaseOrder} /&amp;gt;
 )}
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Design Decision:&lt;/strong&gt; I kept the UI component pure and declarative—each new field is rendered only if a value exists, preserving the existing layout.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  6. End‑to‑End Test
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After the changes, I ran the integration test suite (&lt;code&gt;npm run test:e2e&lt;/code&gt;). The new search paths returned the expected devices, and the detail sheet displayed the invoice data without breaking existing tests.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;PASS src/features/search/GlobalSearch.test.tsx
PASS src/features/devices/DeviceDetailSheet.test.tsx
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;All 124 tests passed.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Key Takeaway
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Validate domain constraints at the source (regex, schema) rather than patching UI checks.&lt;/strong&gt; By aligning the data model, validation logic, and UI rendering, you get a single source of truth that prevents subtle bugs like over‑matching queries and eliminates duplicated validation code.&lt;/p&gt;




