&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Which trees near you are turning color this weekend, and which day should you go out?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Built with PriorLabs-TabPFN.&lt;/strong&gt; peakweek forecasts fall color for 8 common trees in the US Northeast and
Mid-Atlantic from 6,650 iNaturalist community-science observations and this season's weather, using the open
TabPFN v2 weights on a plain CPU. The answer is one card you can print, and then you go outside.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/Bowen1314/peakweek/docs/demo.gif"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2FBowen1314%2Fpeakweek%2FHEAD%2Fdocs%2Fdemo.gif" alt="peakweek demo"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The waiting part (about a minute of TabPFN on a 4-core CPU) is sped up in the GIF. Full video: &lt;a href="https://github.com/Bowen1314/peakweek/docs/demo.mp4" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;docs/demo.mp4&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="markdown-heading"&gt;
&lt;h2 class="heading-element"&gt;How the forecast works&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;peakweek answers "if someone photographs a red maple near me on Saturday, will its leaves be green
colored or gone?" for 8 common fall-color trees (red maple, sugar maple, sweetgum, northern red oak,
American beech, black gum, sassafras, Norway maple) and each of the next 15 days.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Training data.&lt;/strong&gt; Community scientists on iNaturalist annotate tree photos with a…&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="gh-btn-container"&gt;&lt;a class="gh-btn" href="https://github.com/Bowen1314/peakweek" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;View on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;The app itself (server, page, card, verdict logic) uses only the Python standard library. The model side needs &lt;code&gt;tabpfn&lt;/code&gt; and a CPU build of PyTorch. MIT licensed. The TabPFN v2 weights are licensed separately by Prior Labs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The data: people photographing trees
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On iNaturalist, people can tag a tree photo with what its leaves are doing: &lt;em&gt;Green Leaves&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Colored Leaves&lt;/em&gt;, or &lt;em&gt;No Live Leaves&lt;/em&gt;. That makes it a labeled phenology dataset that nobody had to organize. I pulled every annotated September–November observation of the 8 species in a box covering the US Northeast and Mid-Atlantic (latitude 38.5–47.5, longitude −80.5 to −66.9), from 2018 to 2025.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The API returned 10,877 observations, and most of the work was deciding what to throw away:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;dropped&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;observations&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;planted or cultivated trees&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;879&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;"casual" quality grade&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;52&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;obscured coordinates&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;244&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;location accurate to worse than 1 km&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;455&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;conflicting annotations (every one of them "green" and "colored" on the same tree)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;856&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;no usable leaf annotation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;no open license (I don't redistribute these)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,740&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;kept&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;6,650&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That leaves 3,457 green, 2,846 colored and 347 bare observations, all in &lt;code&gt;data/observations.csv&lt;/code&gt; with their iNaturalist ids and license codes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The features: this year's weather, not just the calendar
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The calendar and latitude explain a lot of fall color. The question I cared about is whether &lt;em&gt;this&lt;/em&gt; fall's weather adds anything on top of that. So for every observation, the same code that the app uses (&lt;code&gt;peakweek/features.py&lt;/code&gt;) computes, from Open-Meteo's ERA5 archive:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;chilling degree days since September 1 (the sum of how far each day's mean temperature fell below 20 °C);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;frost nights (≤ 0 °C) and cold nights (≤ 5 °C) since September 1;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;last week's mean minimum temperature and the last two weeks' mean temperature;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;rain over the last 30 days;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;how much warmer or colder this fall has been than the same place's other falls;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;plus species, latitude, longitude, elevation, day of year and day length. Weather comes per 1-degree grid cell. A finer grid would have needed about 15,000 weighted calls to Open-Meteo, more than the free tier allows in a day. The whole project used about 7,000.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One detail I didn't expect: Open-Meteo's forecast API can also return recent past days, which looked like an easy way to get "this season so far". But for September 2026 it returned only about 50 of the 92 past days I asked for, and its rainfall ran 2.3 mm/day below the archive. A model trained on archive weather would have been reading a different instrument. So the app takes September 1 to yesterday from the same ERA5 archive as the training data, and uses the forecast API only for today and the next 14 days.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The model: TabPFN v2, on a CPU
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/PriorLabs/TabPFN" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TabPFN&lt;/a&gt; is a tabular foundation model from Prior Labs. You don't train it on your data. At prediction time you show it a set of labeled rows (the "context") along with the rows you want predicted, and it returns class probabilities with no training step at all. That suits this problem well: a few thousand rows, a mix of categorical and numeric features, and an answer where calibrated probabilities matter more than the top class.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I used the &lt;strong&gt;TabPFN v2&lt;/strong&gt; open weights (&lt;code&gt;Prior-Labs/TabPFN-v2-clf&lt;/code&gt; on Hugging Face, 29 MB, Apache 2.0 plus an attribution requirement). They download without an account and run on a CPU. On a 4-core machine with no GPU, under a hard 800 MB memory limit, one forecast (8 species × 15 days = 120 rows) takes 51–58 seconds once the server is warm (the first forecast after it starts took 99 seconds in the demo recording), using about 670 MB of memory.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The interesting choice is which rows go into the context, since on a CPU, inside 800 MB, about 1,000 rows is the practical limit. I tried four strategies on a validation year:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One context per species&lt;/strong&gt; was the obvious idea, and it broke in a TabPFN-specific way. Some species have almost no "bare" rows (sweetgum has 1 in 180), and when a class is missing from the context, TabPFN gives it near-zero probability. A single bare tree of such a species then costs a fortune in log loss: 0.577 overall, against 0.436 for one shared context. Adding a little mass to the missing classes brought it down to 0.461, still worse.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Nearest neighbours&lt;/strong&gt; in place and week (a separate context for each place and week) was 24 times slower and not measurably better (−0.002 log loss, 95% CI −0.028 to +0.022).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One shared context&lt;/strong&gt; of 1,000 rows, sampled evenly across species × leaf state, did best.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Averaging three such contexts&lt;/strong&gt; (different random samples) beat a single one in 97% of paired bootstrap resamples. That's the final setup: 3 contexts × 4 estimators.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Is it any good? One shot at a held-out year
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I split by year, so the model never sees the fall it's being tested on. I chose every setting above on 2024 (training on 2018–2023), wrote the configuration to &lt;code&gt;eval/locked.json&lt;/code&gt;, and then scored 2025 &lt;strong&gt;once&lt;/strong&gt; (training on 2018–2024; 1,725 observations). Lower is better for log loss and Brier score:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;model, 2025 test&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;log loss&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Brier&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;accuracy&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TabPFN v2, all features&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;0.498&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;0.285&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.805&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;gradient-boosted trees (sklearn), all features&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.505&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.291&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.802&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;TabPFN v2, calendar only (species, place, day of year)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.516&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.291&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.800&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;logistic regression, all features&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.519&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.289&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.805&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;logistic regression, species + day of year + latitude&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.552&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.307&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.795&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;climatology (how often each species was colored that week in past years)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.581&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.345&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.752&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What I take from it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;TabPFN beat the baseline I committed to in advance.&lt;/strong&gt; On the 2024 validation year, logistic regression on the same features was the strongest baseline, and it tied TabPFN almost exactly (0.4305 against 0.4307). On 2025, TabPFN was better by 0.021 log loss: 95% paired-bootstrap CI −0.035 to −0.007 when resampling observations, and −0.041 to −0.003 when resampling grid-cell × week clusters.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;This fall's weather matters.&lt;/strong&gt; The same TabPFN given only species, place and date was 0.018 worse (CI −0.026 to −0.011). Knowing how cold the nights have been beats knowing the date.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It's not a clean sweep.&lt;/strong&gt; Gradient-boosted trees came within 0.007 of TabPFN, which is not a significant difference. Per species, TabPFN was best for red maple, sugar maple and American beech, while simpler models did better on northern red oak, black gum, sweetgum and sassafras (45–164 test observations each). Accuracy is about 0.80 for every decent model, so the gain is in the probabilities, not in the top guess.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Years differ more than models do.&lt;/strong&gt; Every model scored worse on 2025 than on 2024 (logistic regression went from 0.431 to 0.519).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also checked the live pipeline on this fall. There were 99 annotated observations from September 26 to October 5, 2026 that passed the same quality filters (77 green, 22 colored), all made after the training data ends. Logistic regression scored 0.436 and TabPFN 0.454. That's too few observations to rank the models (CI on the difference −0.011 to +0.046), but it shows the app's probabilities are sensible on data nobody has seen. Every run, including the ones that lost, is in &lt;code&gt;eval/RESULTS.md&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  From probabilities to "go outside"
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The card never changes the model's numbers. It only summarizes them, with rules simple enough to print on the card:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;verdict&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;rule (weekend average)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;late&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;chance of bare branches ≥ 35%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;go&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;chance of color ≥ 50%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;late&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;color is already fading: falling since earlier in the window while bare branches rise&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;starting&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;chance of color ≥ 25%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;wait&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;otherwise, with the day color is most likely&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also had to settle on wording. "About 4 in 10 sugar maples you find this weekend should show fall color" is honest, because that's what the model predicts: one tree you come across, not the share of the canopy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first version of the card had a problem you'd only catch by reading it: it told Burlington, Vermont, to go see sweetgum this weekend. The weather side of the forecast wasn't the issue; the model simply has no idea where trees grow. iNaturalist has no research-grade sweetgum records at all within 50 km of Burlington. So for each place, the app now makes one more keyless call to iNaturalist and counts research-grade observations of each tree within 50 km. A tree with fewer than 5 stays on the card with its numbers, but it's listed last, dimmed, marked "Rarely recorded within 50 km", and left out of the headline. In Burlington that moves sweetgum (0 records) and sassafras (3) to the bottom. In New Brunswick, every one of the eight trees has at least 541 records nearby.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  What it can't do yet
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It's better at "not yet" than at "too late".&lt;/strong&gt; People rarely photograph bare trees: only 347 of 6,650 observations are bare. So the chance of bare branches stays small even late in the window, and in early October the best day is often the last day of the forecast. "Late" is rare on the card.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The labels come from what people chose to photograph.&lt;/strong&gt; A brilliant maple gets photographed more often than a dull one.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The data is recent.&lt;/strong&gt; 2024 and 2025 make up 65% of the rows, and 2018 has only 53.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Weather is per 1-degree cell&lt;/strong&gt;, so a cold hollow and a sunny ridge in the same cell get the same weather (elevation is still a feature).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For peakweek, "open" isn't a slogan. Every layer of the project depends on something open:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Open weights mean no gatekeeper and no meter.&lt;/strong&gt; TabPFN v2 is 29 MB that downloads without an account and runs on a 4-core CPU with no GPU, inside 800 MB of memory. A forecast costs nothing, and the model has no API key, no usage limit and no vendor that could switch it off or raise the price. (The weather and the local tree counts do come from Open-Meteo's and iNaturalist's free public APIs.) That's also why the project is pinned to v2: the newer TabPFN checkpoints require a login to download, and I wanted something anyone could clone and run.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Open data made the model possible.&lt;/strong&gt; The training set is 6,650 observations from people who stopped to photograph a tree and tagged its leaves. It's free to query, licensed per observation, and I republish the subset I used with each observation's license code. The weather comes from Open-Meteo under CC BY 4.0. &lt;code&gt;scripts/build_dataset.py&lt;/code&gt; rebuilds it from the public APIs, and every number in this post comes from files in the repo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;I could look inside and fix things.&lt;/strong&gt; Because the model runs in my own process, I could test four ways of building its context, find the "missing class means zero probability" problem, and measure exact memory and timing under a hard cap. A hosted prediction endpoint would only have shown me the outputs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Open data in, open data out.&lt;/strong&gt; The card ends by asking you to log what you saw on iNaturalist. A closed app would keep that observation for itself. peakweek asks you to put it back into the commons it learned from, where next year's model, or anyone else's, can use it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  My Agent Session
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built this with Claude Code, working as a small team of agents: one built the dataset, the features and the evaluation, and ran the TabPFN backtests on a small home server; another built the card, the page and the CLI. A coordinating session wrote the specs and checked the results. Three choices shaped the build:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Only open weights that run without an account&lt;/strong&gt;, on hardware I already have: TabPFN v2 on a small home server's CPU, under an 800 MB memory limit, with no model weights on my laptop.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Freeze the settings before looking at the test year.&lt;/strong&gt; The configuration was written down before 2025 was scored, and the post reports that one score, including where TabPFN didn't win.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Check the card, not just the metrics.&lt;/strong&gt; The sweetgum-in-Vermont problem above didn't show up in any metric. It showed up when someone read the Burlington card the way a person in Burlington would.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;AI disclosure: this project was built with AI coding agents (Claude Code), which wrote the code, ran the evaluation and drafted this post.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Prize Categories
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Best Use of TabPFN.&lt;/strong&gt; TabPFN v2 (open weights, CPU) is the forecasting model: it predicts green, colored or bare for each species and day from 6,650 historical community-science observations and this season's weather, held-out year evaluation included.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deleting a directory in Linux is easy; choosing the right command for what’s inside it takes a little more care. &lt;code&gt;rmdir&lt;/code&gt; stops if anything remains. &lt;code&gt;rm -r&lt;/code&gt; deletes a directory tree, so a typo or unexpected path can remove more than you intended.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Choose the command by what you want removed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For an empty directory, use &lt;code&gt;rmdir&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;rmdir dirname&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It removes the directory only if it is empty. Hidden files count as contents, so a directory that looks empty in a regular &lt;code&gt;ls&lt;/code&gt; listing may still make &lt;code&gt;rmdir&lt;/code&gt; fail.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a directory that contains files or subdirectories, use recursive removal:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;rm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;dirname&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;-r&lt;/code&gt; option tells &lt;code&gt;rm&lt;/code&gt; to descend into the directory and delete its contents before removing the directory itself. This is permanent from the command line, so inspect the target first if there’s any doubt.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;pwd
ls&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-la&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;dirname&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;pwd&lt;/code&gt; confirms your current location; &lt;code&gt;ls -la&lt;/code&gt; shows the target’s contents, including hidden files. This is especially useful over SSH, where a familiar directory name might exist in more than one place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What &lt;code&gt;-f&lt;/code&gt; changes—and what it doesn’t
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You’ll often see this command:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;rm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-rf&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;dirname&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;-r&lt;/code&gt; makes the removal recursive. &lt;code&gt;-f&lt;/code&gt; suppresses confirmation prompts and ignores missing paths. It does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; make &lt;code&gt;rm&lt;/code&gt; recursive by itself: &lt;code&gt;rm -f dirname&lt;/code&gt; still won’t remove a directory containing files.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;-f&lt;/code&gt; only when you intend to skip prompts. If you want to review each removal as it happens, use interactive mode instead:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;rm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-ri&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;dirname&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Be particularly cautious with commands assembled from variables or wildcards. In a script, check that a path variable is set and points where you expect before using it in a destructive command. Don’t rely on &lt;code&gt;rm&lt;/code&gt;’s safeguards to catch every dangerous path construction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There’s also a symlink detail worth knowing: if &lt;code&gt;dirname&lt;/code&gt; is a symbolic link to a directory and you want to remove just the link, use &lt;code&gt;rm dirname&lt;/code&gt; without a trailing slash. A trailing slash can make the path resolve through the link and act on the target’s contents. When you’re unsure what a path refers to, inspect it before deleting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Empty several directories, or clear one in place
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;rmdir&lt;/code&gt; can remove a chain of directories from the end upward, but only while each one is empty:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;rmdir&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-p&lt;/span&gt; parent/child/grandchild
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It tries &lt;code&gt;grandchild&lt;/code&gt;, then &lt;code&gt;child&lt;/code&gt;, then &lt;code&gt;parent&lt;/code&gt;, and stops if a directory still contains something.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want to remove several directory trees, pass their paths to one command:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;rm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-r&lt;/span&gt; dir1 dir2 dir3
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Each path is processed independently; an error on one does not necessarily prevent the others from being processed. Check every path before running the command.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sometimes the goal is to empty a directory while keeping the directory itself—for example, because its ownership or permissions should remain unchanged. Use &lt;code&gt;find&lt;/code&gt; for that:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;find &lt;span class="nb"&gt;dirname&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-mindepth&lt;/span&gt; 1 &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-delete&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;-mindepth 1&lt;/code&gt; skips the starting directory and selects its contents. This also catches hidden files, unlike a simple &lt;code&gt;dirname/*&lt;/code&gt; shell glob, which normally skips names beginning with a dot.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  When deletion fails
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If &lt;code&gt;rmdir&lt;/code&gt; reports that the directory is not empty, inspect it with &lt;code&gt;ls -la dirname&lt;/code&gt;. If you do intend to remove its contents, switch to &lt;code&gt;rm -r&lt;/code&gt; rather than trying to force &lt;code&gt;rmdir&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If &lt;code&gt;rm&lt;/code&gt; says “Is a directory,” you used it without &lt;code&gt;-r&lt;/code&gt;. Use &lt;code&gt;rmdir&lt;/code&gt; for an empty directory or &lt;code&gt;rm -r&lt;/code&gt; when you intend to remove a tree.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A “Permission denied” error may be about the &lt;strong&gt;parent&lt;/strong&gt; directory, not the directory you’re deleting. Removing a directory entry requires appropriate permissions on its parent. Check the parent’s permissions and your current user:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;ls&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-ld&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;whoami
id&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The sticky bit can add ownership restrictions in shared directories such as &lt;code&gt;/tmp&lt;/code&gt;. If you need a refresher on what permission changes mean, this guide to &lt;a href="https://sshflow.com/blog/change-file-permissions-linux/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;changing file permissions in Linux&lt;/a&gt; explains the basics. Avoid broad fixes such as &lt;code&gt;chmod 777&lt;/code&gt;; they can make a directory writable by everyone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Shell commands and SFTP are different
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you’re connected with an SFTP client rather than an interactive shell, don’t assume shell commands work there. SFTP has its own command set: its &lt;code&gt;rmdir&lt;/code&gt; removes an empty remote directory, while removing a non-empty tree may require shell access or a recursive-delete feature in your client. The &lt;a href="https://sshflow.com/blog/sftp-commands/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OpenSSH SFTP command reference&lt;/a&gt; covers the distinction and the available commands.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before a recursive delete, verify the location and target. Use &lt;code&gt;rmdir&lt;/code&gt; when you want Linux to refuse if anything remains; use &lt;code&gt;rm -r&lt;/code&gt; only when you mean to delete the directory and its contents. Reserve &lt;code&gt;-f&lt;/code&gt; for cases where suppressing prompts and ignoring missing paths is intentional.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I originally published a more detailed version of this guide on the &lt;a href="https://sshflow.com/blog/remove-directory-linux/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SSHFlow blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm also building &lt;a href="https://sshflow.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SSHFlow&lt;/a&gt; — an SSH client where every server gets its own workspace for terminals, SFTP, code, and databases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TL;DR: Count terminal notification delivery failures, query a recent window on a schedule, and notify only when a threshold crossing changes durable alert state. The metric answers an aggregate question; a Postgres row answers the equally important question of whether this worker has already announced that condition. Keep those responsibilities separate, and use a heartbeat monitor for the different failure in which the polling job never runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a marketplace notification service, my decision rule is concrete: use a counter-and-poller design when the operational question is “did at least &lt;em&gt;N&lt;/em&gt; deliveries fail in this window?” Choose a native alerting product when routing, escalation, grouping, and on-call policy are themselves requirements. The first design is small and auditable, but only if retries cannot produce duplicate pages and a recovery transition is recorded as carefully as a firing transition.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How should a cron worker poll metrics for a failure count alert?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A delivery attempt can be retried, yet the counter should represent the event the team intends to operate on. Counting every transient attempt creates noise; counting only a notification that has exhausted its delivery policy measures the terminal outcome. That distinction belongs at the producer, close to the state transition, rather than in an alert query that tries to reconstruct intent later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use names such as &lt;code&gt;job_failures&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;api_5xx&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;payment_failures&lt;/code&gt;, with bounded tags such as service and environment. For this system I would emit a delivery-failure counter after the notification reaches its terminal failed state. I would not tag it with recipient, message ID, or arbitrary error text: those values belong in an audit record, while a counter needs a controlled aggregation boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Noise compounds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three invariants follow. A logical terminal failure increments once. A given threshold episode produces at most one initial notification during its cooldown. Every decision can be reconstructed from the queried window, configured threshold, prior state, and delivery result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Exactly once is an aspiration across a database, a metrics service, and a notification provider, not a property granted by a cron expression. The practical design is an idempotent producer plus durable alert state. If the increment occurs beside a business transaction, an outbox record with a unique event identifier gives a retryable handoff; if the metric sink cannot deduplicate that identifier, reconciliation against the authoritative delivery table remains necessary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Decision and failure boundaries
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The scheduled worker owns the threshold, lookback window, cooldown, and notification delivery because a metrics query surface alone does not supply a native alert engine or notification channels. Its cycle is short: read the recent aggregate, lock the alert-state row, decide whether this observation is a new firing or recovery transition, deliver the notification, and persist the result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not quietly assume query filters. The exact filtering parameters for &lt;code&gt;metrics.query&lt;/code&gt; are not clearly declared in discovery, so validate the service-and-environment query against the live discovery schema and a known data set before depending on it. Infrai is one option for that narrow role, and its advantage here is a plain REST API: there is no SDK to install or client-library version to babysit, so anything that can send an HTTP request can call it in any language. Its self-describing public discovery surface exposes request and response schemas without a key, making schema validation an explicit deployment check rather than folklore. Infrai also uses one API key and one bill across 295 routes in 20 modules, so a service already using another backend capability does not acquire another credential and invoice reconciliation path merely to publish a counter; that breadth does not remove the alert-state design described here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are four failure boundaries worth naming:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The notification service commits a terminal failure but does not report it. An outbox and reconciliation repair this boundary.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The metric is present but the polling worker does not run. A separate heartbeat monitor detects this silent failure.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The worker observes a breach but crashes around notification delivery. An idempotency key at the delivery boundary, or a transactional notification outbox, prevents duplicate application.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The worker runs repeatedly while the value remains high. Durable state and cooldown policy prevent repeated noise.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One subtle limit matters for regulated workflows: aggregate metrics are not an audit ledger. They are evidence for an operational decision, while the immutable delivery transition, actor or process, timestamps, and notification outcome should remain in the system of record under its applicable retention and access controls. There is also no user-scoped log deletion interface in this metrics-and-logs option, so personal data should not be placed in metric tags; GDPR erasure obligations cannot be delegated to a counter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Option comparison
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The products below solve overlapping, not identical, problems. A fair choice turns on how much alert policy the team wants to own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Best fit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What the team still owns&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Important boundary&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;REST metrics plus a scheduled worker&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A small number of aggregate thresholds where plain REST and no client-library lifecycle are valuable&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Query validation, thresholds, durable state, cooldowns, and all notification routing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No native alert engine or notification channel; no heartbeat monitoring&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Prometheus plus Alertmanager&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Teams already operating Prometheus-style rules and wanting alert grouping, inhibition, silences, and routing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Metric correctness, rule maintenance, and the availability of the monitoring stack&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;More infrastructure and policy surface than a small poller&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Datadog Monitors&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Teams wanting a managed monitor and notification workflow around collected telemetry&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Instrumentation, monitor tuning, access policy, and vendor configuration&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A broader managed platform commitment&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Sentry issue alerts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Application failures that naturally appear as grouped error events and need issue-oriented workflows&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Capture quality, grouping behavior, and alert-rule tuning&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Less direct for a business counter such as terminal delivery failures&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Healthchecks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Detecting that a cron job or scheduled worker did not report on time&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The job's check-in and escalation configuration&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Complements failure counters; it does not replace their threshold query&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Signal quality is the governing axis. Prometheus with Alertmanager is stronger when teams need mature routing policy under their control; Datadog is stronger when a managed monitoring plane already exists; Sentry is a natural fit when exception grouping is the operational unit. The REST-and-worker option stays attractive when the rule set is deliberately small and the team values a narrow dependency, but its simplicity disappears if engineers start rebuilding rotations, silences, escalation chains, or complex multi-window logic. This is a real limitation: the metrics service does not support the native alert rules or notification routing required by those cases, and it is not suitable as a substitute for Alertmanager or Datadog Monitors when those policies are central. The trade-off is explicit ownership of a small worker in exchange for a smaller integration surface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Choose accordingly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The critical path in Go
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The example below is runnable with the standard library. It isolates the metric query and notification adapters because the exact query parameters must be confirmed rather than guessed. The state transition itself is the part that must remain stable across adapters: it models firing, cooldown suppression, and recovery, and it derives a deterministic delivery key from the alert name and episode start.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;package&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
